  "startup_note": "navigation → the first frame that shows the whole 10,062-word document, median of 12 cold loads (editor ready at 71 ms). An empty document paints at 56 ms. Launching the whole app with bin/quill on the real display — chromium process spawn, window, fonts, full render — was 395 and 511 ms to pixels.",
  "bar_keystroke": 32.5,
  "bar_note": "iA Writer publishes no latency numbers at all. Bar from third-party measurements of native editors: Sublime Text 32.5 ± 4.0 ms keyboard-to-photon (Hume, photodiode, 60 Hz), TextEdit 33.4, Atom 45.6, VS Code 47.6; app-internal input→paint ≤5 ms (Fatin's Typometer). Quill's estimated keyboard-to-photon is ≈32 ms: 13.8 ms measured on a real 60 Hz panel + 14 ms keyboard + 4 ms pixel response (the last two Fatin's published budget, cited not measured). App-internal work per keystroke: 2.94 ms to committed frame, 4.89 ms of main-thread busy time — inside the ≤5 ms bar.",
  "method": "Per keystroke, from Chrome's own EventTiming trace records (devtools.timeline, unrounded, µs): event hardware timestamp → presentation feedback of the frame carrying it. 300 keystrokes per regime, five regimes, 90 ms pacing (~133 wpm), into a real 10,062-word Markdown document with the caret scrolled on screen; 25 warm-up keys discarded; every keystroke accounted for (keys pressed = keydowns in the trace = keys resolved by an independent in-page rAF probe). Headless Chromium 151.0.7922.173, i5-13600K, with a 1 px composited animation keeping the frame clock running so headless models a 60 Hz panel instead of producing frames on demand (that alone is worth ~6 ms and is how most browser latency numbers flatter themselves). Headline is the hardest paced regime (caret in the middle of the draft); at the end of the draft it is 9.17 / 18.5 ms. With the display clock removed — the application's own cost, comparable to Typometer — 4.23 ms p50 / 6.14 p99. Measured on a real 60 Hz panel (Dell U2720Q, Wayland), an earlier build measured 13.83 / 29.13 ms including the scanout hop; that is quoted as the upper bound in the report. Full method and caveats: progress/latency-report.md.",
